argument:
@drawrow recomputes rc_sub from rc_y's parity on EVERY call, and the caller then reads rc_sub back to decide whether a second char row belongs to the same tile row. But the parity is not free information: the loop already knows it. The first @drawrow of a tile row is even unless the rect itself started odd, and after @nextrow rc_y has been incremented twice, so it is always even again; and the @second call is always the odd half. Hoist the one real test to the top of the tile-row loop (it is where the caller's 'lda rc_sub / bne @nextrow' test was doing the same job) and have each arm write rc_sub and rowoff directly, so @drawrow no longer needs lines 488-494 at all.
Cycle count, per tile row with both char rows drawn (the normal case):
  old: call 1 (rc_y even) lda rc_y 3 + and #1 2 + beq taken 3 + sta rc_sub 3 + ora rc_ro0 3 + sta rowoff 3 = 17;
       caller lda rc_sub 3 + bne not taken 2 = 5;
       call 2 (rc_y odd) 3 + 2 + beq not taken 2 + lda #32 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 18.  Total 40.
  new: top lda rc_y 3 + and #1 2 + bne not taken 2 + stz rc_sub 3 + lda rc_ro0 3 + sta rowoff 3 = 16;
       @second lda #32 2 + sta rc_sub 3 + ora rc_ro0 3 + sta rowoff 3 = 11.  Total 27.
  Saving 13 cycles per tile row.  For the odd-start tile row (only ever the first of a rect) old = 18 + 3 + 3(bne taken) = 24, new = 3 + 2 + 3(bne taken) + 11 = 19, saving 5.  This is the routine's main path: every drawrect draws at least one tile row, and draw_dirty (engine.s:2865) issues one 4x2 rect - exactly one tile row - per dirty cell, so the count is tile rows per frame, not rects.
Correctness: the fall-through into @second replaces 'lda rc_sub / bne @nextrow' exactly - that test could only be true on the odd-start path, which now branches straight to @second and so never reaches the fall-through. rowoff is clobbered inside @drawrow at line 620, so it must be (and is) rewritten before each jsr. A is irrelevant at @nextrow (it starts 'lda ptr') and C is set by the following clc. X and Y are untouched by the new code. Both new branches are well inside range (15 and 43 bytes).
ANONYMOUS LABEL WARNING: this deletes the ':' at line 492 together with its only reference, the 'beq :+' at line 490. I checked the whole file region: the nearest preceding forward references ('bne :+' at 382, 'bcc :+' at 394) both resolve to the ':' at line 396, and there is no ':-' anywhere between line 492 and the next ':' (line 507), so no other branch counts across the pair. Byte cost +2 (18 bytes of old code become 20).

